      "generated_answer": "Research on stable carbon isotopes indicates that early hominids transitioned from a diet primarily consisting of C3 resources (such as forest-derived fruits, nuts, and leaves) to one incorporating C4 resources (such as tropical grasses and sedges, or animals that ate them). This shift reflects the expansion of savanna environments and a broadening of the hominid niche. Therefore, the transition was from C3-based foods like fruits and nuts to C4-based grass resources.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
