    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A more accurate calibration curve and conversion for radiocarbon dating can be derived from:\n\nOptions: \nA. coins recovered from ancient shipwrecks.\nB. dendrochronology.\nC. all of the above.\nD. ice core sampling.\nE. written records.\nF. bone dating of ancient human remains.\nG. examination of sediment layers.\nH. satellite imagery.\nI. fossil records.\nJ. carbon dating of historical buildings.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most accurate calibration curves for radiocarbon dating are derived from **dendrochronology** (tree-ring dating), as it provides a year-by-year record of atmospheric carbon-14 levels. By comparing the radiocarbon age of a specific tree ring to its known calendar age, scientists can correct for historical fluctuations in carbon isotopes. While other methods like sediment layers or ice cores are used, tree rings remain the primary standard for high-precision calibration.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
