    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4652",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nRead the following excerpt.\nNever were talents of the highest genius of the most exalted kind, more profusely bestowed upon a human being. The genius of Napoleon is astounding. All branches of human knowledge seemed alike familiar to his gigantic mind. His conversations at St. Helena, scattered through the numerous and voluminous memorials of those who gleaned them, are replete with intensest interest. During the long agony of his imprisonment and his death, he conversed with perfect freedom upon the events of his marvelous career, and upon all those subjects or morals, politics, and religion, which most deeply concern the welfare of our race. There is no mind which will not be invigorated by familiarity with these profound thoughts, expressed with so much glow of feeling and energy of diction.\n—John S. C. Abbott, historian, Napoleon at St. Helena, 1855\nWhich of the following actions of Napoleon does the author seem to be ignoring the hypocrisy of in his examination of the high mindedness and praiseworthiness of Napoleon?\n\nOptions: \nA. His manipulation of the press for political gain\nB. His conquest of Europe and use of the Continental System\nC. His imposition of French culture on conquered territories\nD. The invasion of Haiti to stop a slave revolt there\nE. His establishment of the Bank of France\nF. His self-appointment as Emperor of France\nG. Promoting soldiers and government workers based upon their merits only\nH. The creation of the Napoleonic Code\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **D**.\n\nThe author praises Napoleon’s \"exalted\" genius and his concern for \"morals, politics, and religion, which most deeply concern the welfare of our race.\" This creates a stark hypocrisy when contrasted with Napoleon's decision to reinstitute slavery and invade Haiti to suppress a slave revolt, an action that directly contradicts the Enlightenment ideals of liberty and human welfare the author attributes to him. While other options represent political or military strategies, the invasion of Haiti represents a fundamental moral contradiction to the \"high-mindedness\" described in the text.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
