    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What do anthropologists NOT need to know to determine when people entered the Americas from Siberia?\n\nOptions: \nA. When sled dogs were first domesticated\nB. When eastern Siberia was first inhabited\nC. When the Bering Strait was exposed and open for travel\nD. The timeline of the Ice Age\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine when people entered the Americas from Siberia, anthropologists primarily focus on geological conditions (the Bering Land Bridge), the timing of glacial cycles, and the presence of human populations in the source region. While sled dogs were eventually used in the Arctic, they were not a prerequisite for the initial migration, which likely occurred on foot or via coastal routes.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
