    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4758",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The greatest contribution that Africa can make to the peace of the world is to avoid all the dangers inherent in disunity, by creating a political union which will also by its success, stand as an example to a divided world. A Union of African states will project more effectively the African personality. It will command respect from a world that has regard only for size and influence. The scant attention paid to African opposition to the French atomic tests in the Sahara, and the ignominious spectacle of the U.N. in the Congo quibbling about constitutional niceties while the Republic was tottering into anarchy, are evidence of the callous disregard of African Independence by the Great Powers.\"\nKwame Nkrumah, Ghanian leader, 1961\nBased on the speech, the French saw Africa as which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. A continent that needs French intervention to maintain peace\nB. Important due to the Sahara's natural resources\nC. An example of successful political union\nD. A resource for enhancing French power in the Cold War\nE. An obstacle to French atomic tests in the Sahara\nF. A strong political union\nG. A threat to French power and influence\nH. A continent too divided to be of significance\nI. A collection of republics tottering into anarchy\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Based on the text, Nkrumah argues that the \"scant attention\" paid to African opposition to French atomic tests proves that the Great Powers (including France) had a \"callous disregard\" for African independence. This implies that the French viewed the continent as too fragmented and weak to offer significant resistance or command respect. Therefore, the French saw Africa as a region whose lack of unity rendered it insignificant on the global stage.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
