    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is based on the historical context of the 19th-century British monarchy, where the sovereign's role had shifted from direct governance to a more ceremonial and social figurehead. The letter highlights that while Victoria is exhausted by social obligations, she defers to Lord Melbourne (the Prime Minister) on matters of protocol, and notes that political negotiations with the Tories occur independently of these festivities. This suggests that the monarch's schedule was dominated by social ritual while substantive political power resided with Parliament and the Prime Minister.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
